  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я знаю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I know.' The key word here means 'I', which Max combines with 'know'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Тебе вызвать такси?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Should I call you a taxi?' Notice the word order: 'for you' comes first, and 'taxi' finishes the sentence.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Зачем?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Why?' In Russian, 'what for' leads into 'what for' in a very natural everyday phrasing. And Sasha jumps in.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Ты сегодня без машины.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You are without a car today.' Sasha puts 'you' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'today'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "sasha",
   "t": "Как ты будешь добираться домой?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'How will you get home?' Sasha starts with 'how' and pairs it with 'you' to make the point clear.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я поеду на метро.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
